# TODO(owner, M4 OQ-2): THIS FILE HAS NO KEY YET, ON PURPOSE. No release can be verified, and the
# release workflow warns, until the owner adds the release identity's public key here in a PR to main.
#
# The format is ssh-keygen's allowed-signers file. One line per key and namespace, ONE KEY PER LINE,
# no private key ever:
#
#   release namespaces="git"          ssh-ed25519 AAAA...    (verifies the signed tag: git verify-tag)
#   release namespaces="fil-release"  ssh-ed25519 AAAA...    (verifies SHA256SUMS.sig, ssh-keygen -Y verify -n fil-release)
#
# `just release-verify` reads this file from MAIN, never from the tag being checked, so a key added
# only on a tag's own commit verifies nothing (tests/release/verify-tag.sh). Rotating the key is a PR
# to main; a tag signed by a retired key stops verifying once main drops it.
